      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, and the relationship runs both directions. Low testosterone can make sleep worse, and poor sleep lowers testosterone production, since most testosterone is produced during deep sleep. That's part of why we evaluate the two together rather than treating either in isolation."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is TRT the only treatment for low testosterone?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. TRT is one possible treatment, and it's the right one for some men — but it isn't the starting assumption for every man who comes in with symptoms. We recommend it only after a full evaluation shows it's the right fit. Depending on what the labs show, the plan may focus on sleep, stress, or metabolic health before or alongside TRT."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does RHM only treat testosterone, or will I automatically be put on TRT?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. We evaluate your hormones, metabolism, stress, sleep, and inflammation together before recommending anything. TRT is one possible outcome of that evaluation, not the default. If your labs point somewhere else — or a different combination of changes is what's actually driving how you feel — that's what we'll recommend instead."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What's the difference between andropause and normal aging?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Andropause refers to the gradual decline in testosterone that can happen with age, but it isn't the same thing as normal aging on its own. The distinction matters: andropause is evaluable and often treatable, while writing symptoms off as \"just getting older\" can mean missing something that responds well to the right plan."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does a comprehensive men's hormone panel actually test?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A comprehensive panel goes beyond total testosterone. It typically includes free testosterone, estradiol, DHEA-S, cortisol, thyroid markers, fasting insulin, and inflammatory markers, since these systems influence each other. Testing only one number gives you one data point, not the full picture."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I need a referral to be evaluated for low testosterone?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Generally, no — you can schedule an evaluation directly. If you're already working with a specialist for a related condition, we can work alongside that care rather than in place of it."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is metabolic health?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Metabolic health refers to how efficiently your body regulates blood sugar, insulin, and energy use. When these systems work well, your body can access and burn stored fat efficiently; when they're not, the same diet and exercise effort often produces a smaller result."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does poor metabolic health look like in men?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It can show up as stalled weight loss despite consistent effort, fat that concentrates around the midsection, energy crashes after meals, stronger sugar or carb cravings, and muscle that's harder to build or keep. Symptoms alone can't confirm what's driving it — a lab panel is the only way to know."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is metabolic health assessed or tested?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Evaluation typically includes fasting insulin, HbA1c, a lipid panel, and hormone and thyroid markers, since these systems influence each other. A single blood sugar reading gives you one data point, not the full picture."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can metabolic health be improved without medication?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "For some men, addressing sleep, stress, and training load can meaningfully shift these markers. For others, the evaluation points toward additional support. We don't start from a fixed answer — the plan follows what your labs show, always alongside your physician's guidance."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does metabolic health get harder to maintain with age?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Hormone levels, muscle mass, and insulin sensitivity tend to shift gradually with age, which can make the same diet and exercise routine less effective over time. That shift is evaluable, not something you have to just accept — it's part of why we test for it directly instead of assuming it."
      }
    }
  ]
}

